CMK is an *extensible language*.  There's [a few different aspects to that]({{_}}/cmk/paradigm/#language-properties) including support for [macros, templating, and constructors]({{_}}/cmk/banana) that are powerful enough to implement [classes and metaclasses]({{_}}/demos/oop), but this page is focused on other aspects of the **metaprogramming playground.**  

What does that even mean?  Well, besides just making stuff by using the language, there are several ways to reshape the language itself.  CMK isn't actually *one* language, but rather a language family.  Add to that the [support for polyglots]({{_}}/demos/polyglots) or [DSLs]({{_}}/cmk/dsls).. and maybe you could say that *language itself* is supported as a primitive.  The rest of this page is a guided tour of this layer.

{# The unifying piece is [banana forms]({{_}}/cmk/banana).  The {{ cmkmath.imath("(|@body|)") }} block is a generic, native *declare*, and it composes with streams, args, env-prefixes, currying, and compile-twice "cooking".  That makes bananas more than a compiler nicety: they are the through-line that ties the rest of this layer together.  You can see how far it reaches in the [`banana-oop` class demo]({{_}}/cmk/banana/#banana-cooked), where cooked {{ cmkmath.imath("(|..|)") }} mixins compose into a small object system *with inheritance*, built entirely from the primitives on this page.  It is the kind of thing that may eventually earn promotion to core language support. #}

* **[Bananas](#bananas)**: block-structured native declares.
* **[DSLs](#dsls)**: guest-language fragments (jqlang / awklang).
* **[Sugar](#about-sugar)**: block-structured rewrites that add semantics to a block.
* **[Dialects](#about-dialects)**: simple *paired* (token) rewrites.
* **[Pragma](#pragma)**: per-file compiler settings.

{{ macros.section(3, "Banana Blocks", id="bananas") }}

!!! terminology "Banana"
    CMK's block-structured *quote*, available in many forms that are documented in more detail [here]({{_}}/cmk/banana), but here's the quick version.  A banana block captures the body and optionally names it, reformats it, cooks it, or passes it into other constructors.  That means it can hold recipes, macro values, or a whole guest-language program.

Bananas are simple and subtle at the same time, and turn out to be the foundational layer for lots of other metaprogramming.  As *the primary lazy multi-liner*, they are less structured than anything with a real AST, but also more structured than a templated string.  Bananas *do* actually get a [string algebra]({{_}}/cmk/quickref/#string-algebra) that allows for templating, but also an [ambient algebra]({{_}}/cmk/concepts/#ambient-algebra), and [their own algebra]({{_}}/cmk/concepts/#callform-algebra).  See also [these demos]({{_}}/cmk/banana).

To get a feel for how much you can actually build with something like this, see also the [object-oriented programming demos]({{_}}/cmk/banana/#banana-cooked), which show how this is enough to build inheritance, mixins, classes, and metaclasses.  

{{ macros.section(3, "DSLs", id="dsls") }}

!!! terminology "DSL"
    A **DSL** [creates a sub-language]({{_}}/cmk/concepts/#dsls): an instance's banana body is written in a guest language (`jq`, `awk`, ...) rather than `make`, folded back into the same compile-time machinery as the rest of this layer.

`dsl` is a member of the `cmk` prelude (`from cmk import dsl`): the built-in kinds live under `dsl.*`, used qualified (`dsl.jqlang`, `dsl.awklang`), and two guest languages ship in the box.  `dsl` is not itself an openable module.

{{ macros.see_also([
    ['DSLs', '@/cmk/dsls', 'the `dsl` prelude member, jqlang / awklang, and the fragment algebra, with demos'],
], mkdocs) }}

{{ macros.section(3, "Sugar", id="about-sugar") }}

!!! terminology "Sugar"
    Sugar is the block-structured cousin of [dialects](#about-dialects).  Whereas a dialect is a flat token rewrite, a sugar rule matches a whole block and **gives it additional semantics**.  Whereas [bananas](#bananas) are the language-native version of this, exposing custom sugars at the file level allows for things like *user-defined syntax* or module-specific syntax.

**Sugar is an array of triples: a start marker, an end marker, and a template.**  It's not magic.. the default values are bundled with the compiler as flat data you can view (see below, and see also [the appendix](#appendix-default-sugar)).  If you dump the data, you'll notice that the default sugar actually shows part of how banana-blocks are implemented:

```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./compose.mk mk.def.read/lang.comp.sugar
```
```bash {.cli_output}
[
	["(|", "|)", "__GENERIC__"]
]
```

{# Sugar **gives matched blocks additional semantics**.  Since each rewritten block has a name, every sugar also requires a name. #}

For the `sugar_template` element of the triple, we render it and add it after the block.  See the table below for an explanation of the variables that may appear inside this template.

{% include "includes/sugar-template-vars.md" %}

**Sugar markers are regexes.** Unlike [dialect patterns](#about-dialects), which match literally, the start and end delimiters are matched as regular expressions against each line. That is by design: it makes sugar a small block-grammar rather than a fixed-token matcher, so a single rule can open a whole *family* of blocks and anchor them to a position on the line. The [regex sugar demo below](#user-defined-sugar) puts this to work. The trade-off is that regex metacharacters keep their special meaning inside a marker, so (as with dialects) unicode glyphs make the safest delimiters whenever you do not want regex behavior.

{{ macros.section(3, "Dialects", id="about-dialects") }}
<a name=dialects></a>

!!! terminology "Dialect"
    A dialect is an array of paired substitution rules, and during compilation these rules take effect everywhere, *except* inside [banana](#bananas) block bodies (and anything that behaves like one).

Using the [reflection capabilities]({{_}}/standard-lib#reflection-support), we can show the compiler's default dialect table (definitional data under `lang.comp.*`, like sugar above) like this:

```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./compose.mk mk.def.read/lang.comp.dialect
[
  ["ᐉ", ".dispatch/"],
  ["🡆", "${stream.stdin} | ${jq} -r"],
  ["🡄", "${jb}"],
  ["__target_name__", "${@}"],
  ["__target__", "this.__target_name__"],
  ["this.", "${make} "],
]
```

**Dialect substitution is literal.** Each pattern is matched as plain text, never a regex, so a `.` is just a dot and a keyword can never accidentally match more than it spells. Preferring unicode symbols is still a good habit, since it guarantees a new keyword won't collide with anything else in your source. (Sugar markers, [covered above](#about-sugar), are the deliberate exception: those *are* regexes.)

**Isolation Property:**  Since substitution does not apply inside block bodies, a new keyword like `this` cannot affect [polyglots]({{_}}/demos/polyglots) even if they also use the same keyword.  You only need to think carefully about collision with shell-commands and their arguments that appear inside normal targets.  If you want to use [CMK Modules]({{_}}/cmk/modules) you can even avoid that.

#### Default Dialect
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

Two more conveniences in the default dialect above are a related pair, both referring to the recipe's own target:

* **`__target_name__`** is the bare target *name*.  It lets a recipe refer to the running target without the `$`-noise (and reads naturally in [CMK call-forms]({{_}}/cmk/concepts/#callforms)), which is handy for per-target naming, e.g. using the target name as a [stage]({{_}}/stages/#stage-idioms) name.
* **`__target__`** is the running target as a *callable*: a recursive self-send.  It composes with the [stream call-form]({{_}}/cmk/concepts/#callforms), so piping a command into it runs that command against a fresh invocation of the current target, while a bare reference re-invokes it.

Full dialect details are available in the [appendix of this page](#appendix-default-dialect), but the rules shown above are the ones used in the [basic example]({{_}}/cmk/compiler/#basic-example).  All we've done so far is introduce new syntax for `jq`, `jb`, the current target name, and recursive self-invocation.

<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

It might not be obvious how useful and powerful simple substitution actually is here, so let's take a brief detour to peel back the layers.  Above is the dialect literal that only does a find/replace on your behalf, **but** it's stacked on top of other templating and macros.  Below you can see a first approximation of variable expansion.

As discussed in [structured IO docs]({{_}}/standard-lib/#structured-io-basics), we try to use local tools if available, and fall back to docker.  Expansion shows that working:

```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./compose.mk mk.get/lang.comp.dialect
[
  [
    "🡆",
    "cat /dev/stdin | /usr/bin/jq -r"
  ],
  [
    "🡄",
    "docker container run --rm ghcr.io/h4l/json.bash/jb:${JB_CLI_VERSION:-0.2.2}",
  ],
  ...
]
```

One layer of expansion reveals that fallback works as advertised; in this case `compose.mk` knows that a local copy of `jq` is available already, but `jb` is missing and will have to use docker.

It also reveals that there is actually yet another layer of expansion, where `JB_CLI_VERSION` has a default value for tool versions that supports user-override.  Therefore *besides* being visually easy to parse and clarifying intent, the **usage of the arrow operators results in portable code with no extra tool installation.**

{{ macros.section(3, "Pragma", id="pragma") }}

!!! terminology "Pragma"
    A **pragma** is a leading-comment JSON hint that sets *per-program settings* in-source.  Where [dialects](#about-dialects) and [sugar](#about-sugar) change how source is *rewritten*, a pragma changes how the compiler is *configured* for this file.

The third member of that family covers the [recipe-join]({{_}}/cmk/compiler/#recipe-body-joining) connector, extra compile stages, boot/lifecycle handlers, and even the program's execution mode.  The full reference (the `cmk_pragma :::` marker, the `__pragma__` resolvers, two-pass plugin stacking, and the boot/`repl` settings) lives on its own page.

{{ macros.see_also([
    ['Compiler Pragmas', '@/cmk/pragmas', 'the marker, resolution rules (pragma &gt; env &gt; default), and the settings catalog'],
], mkdocs) }}

This completes the **dialect + sugar + pragma** triad: three in-source ways to bend the compiler to a single file.

### User-Defined Extensions
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

Both dialects and sugar can be defined at runtime on a per-file basis, basically allowing the code itself to describe aspects of the compiler that will be used on it.

!!! warning "Overwrites, not appends"
    File hints must occur after the shebang, before the first non-comment line, and must be legal JSON that specifies the full dialect/sugar to use.  This *overwrites* the default dialect/sugar, and does not append it!

Minified JSON is optional, and everything between the first and second `:::` will be used, so multi-line JSON is allowed, but must remain comment-prefixed.

